{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788204000973,"sessionID":"ses_fa6bfda21ffejsIdYbHxpf8aCL","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_ec1db74ed9714fdfa0072738","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"python3 -c \"\nimport csv\nwith open('data/kaggle/fifa_data.csv', encoding='utf-8') as fh:\n    r = csv.DictReader(fh)\n    nat = {}\n    clubs = {}\n    n = 0\n    for row in r:\n        n += 1\n        nat[row['Nationality']] = nat.get(row['Nationality'],0)+1\n        c = row['Club']\n        clubs[c] = clubs.get(c,0)+1\nprint('rows', n, 'cols', len(row))\nprint('Brazilians:', nat.get('Brazil'))\nbr = {c:v for c,v in clubs.items() if any(w in c for w in ['Flamengo','Palmeiras','Corinthians','Santos','So Paulo','Sao Paulo','Gremio','Fluminense','Vasco','Bahia','Sport','Athletico','Atltico','Botafogo','Cruzeiro','Internacional','Fortaleza','Bragantino','Cuiab'])}\nprint(sorted(br.items()))\nprint('Overall dtype sample:', row['Overall'])\n\" "},"output":"rows 18207 cols 89\nBrazilians: 827\n[('Bahia', 20), ('Botafogo', 20), ('Ceará Sporting Club', 20), ('Clube Sport Marítimo', 23), ('Cruzeiro', 20), ('Fluminense', 20), ('Internacional', 20), ('Real Sporting de Gijón', 30), ('Santos', 20), ('Santos Laguna', 26), ('Sport Club do Recife', 20), ('Sporting CP', 30), ('Sporting Kansas City', 27), ('Sporting Lokeren', 28), ('Sporting de Charleroi', 28), ('VfL Sportfreunde Lotte', 28)]\nOverall dtype sample: 46\n","metadata":{"output":"rows 18207 cols 89\nBrazilians: 827\n[('Bahia', 20), ('Botafogo', 20), ('Ceará Sporting Club', 20), ('Clube Sport Marítimo', 23), ('Cruzeiro', 20), ('Fluminense', 20), ('Internacional', 20), ('Real Sporting de Gijón', 30), ('Santos', 20), ('Santos Laguna', 26), ('Sport Club do Recife', 20), ('Sporting CP', 30), ('Sporting Kansas City', 27), ('Sporting Lokeren', 28), ('Sporting de Charleroi', 28), ('VfL Sportfreunde Lotte', 28)]\nOverall dtype sample: 46\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"python3 -c \"\nimport csv\nwith open('data/kaggle/fifa_data.csv', encoding='utf-8') as fh:\n    r = csv.DictReader(fh)\n    nat = {}\n    clubs = {}\n    n = 0\n    for row in r:\n        n += 1\n        nat[row['Nationality']] = nat.get(row['Nationality'],0)+1\n        c = row['Club']\n        clubs[c] = clubs.get(c,0)+1\nprint('rows', n, 'cols', len(row))\nprint('Brazilians:', nat.get('Brazil'))\nbr = {c:v for c,v in clubs.items() if any(w in c for w in ['Flamengo','Palmeiras','Corinthians','Santos','So Paulo','Sao Paulo','Gremio','Fluminense','Vasco','Bahia','Sport','Athletico','Atltico','Botafogo','Cruzeiro','Internacional','Fortaleza','Bragantino','Cuiab'])}\nprint(sorted(br.items()))\nprint('Overall dtype sample:', row['Overall'])\n\" ","time":{"start":1788204000969,"end":1788204000971}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have a good picture. Key normalization challenges:\n\n1. State suffixes: \"Palmeiras-SP\", \"América - MG\" (with spaces), \"Bahia - BA\"\n2. Accents: \"Sao Paulo\" vs \"São Paulo\", \"Avai\" vs \"Avaí\", \"Gremio\" vs \"Grêmio\"\n3. Aliases: \"Athletico-PR\"/\"Atlético-PR\"/\"Athletico Paranaense\"/\"Atlético Paranaense\"/\"Athletico\"; \"Vasco\"/\"Vasco da Gama\"/\"Vasco Da Gama RJ\"; \"Atletico-MG\"/\"Atlético-MG\"/\"Atletico Mineiro\"/\"Atlético Mineiro\"; \"Sport-PE\"/\"Sport\"/\"Sport Recife\"; \"Csa-AL\"/\"CSA\"; \"America-MG\"/\"America - MG\"/\"América-MG\"/\"America MG\"\n\nNormalization strategy for canonical key:\n- lowercase\n- strip accents (unicodedata NFD)\n- remove content in parentheses like \"(antigo ...)\", \"(URU)\", \"(PAR)\" — wait, \"(URU)\" is a country disambiguator for foreign teams (Nacional (URU)). Removing parens content: \"Nacional (URU)\" → \"nacional\". Hmm that could collide with \"Nacional AM\" — no, \"Nacional AM\" keeps \"am\". Collisions: \"Guaraní (PAR)\" → \"guarani\" and \"Guaraní-PAR\" → strip \"-par\" suffix → \"guarani\". Good, they should match each other. But careful — Brazilian teams with state suffix \"Atletico-PR\" → strip \"-pr\" → \"atletico\". And \"Atlético-GO\" → \"atletico\" too! Collision between Atletico-PR and Atletico-GO! So blanket stripping of the state suffix is dangerous.\n\nBetter approach: strip state suffix only when it's a Brazilian state abbreviation at the END (two-letter uppercase in original, or known list), then apply alias map to resolve known collisions:\n- \"atletico-pr\" → Atlético Paranaense\n- \"atletico-mg\" → Atlético Mineiro\n- \"atletico-go\" → Atlético Goianiense\n- \"america-mg\" → América Mineiro, \"america-rn\" → América de Natal\n- etc.\n\nSo the plan:\n1. Trim whitespace, collapse spaces.\n2. Remove parenthesized qualifiers (country codes and \"(antigo Esporte Clube Barreira)\").\n3. If ends with a Brazilian state abbreviation after a dash (with/without spaces), strip it and record the state.\n4. Normalize case + accents for the key.\n5. Apply alias dictionary for known variants (checked on the stripped/accent-folded key).\n6. Fallback: the accent-folded lowercase name is the key.\n\nFor collisions after stripping (like \"atletico\"), the alias map handles the famous ones. For obscure small clubs that collapse, there could be rare collisions (e.g., \"Atletico - AC\" (Acre) → \"atletico\" vs \"Atlético - PR\" → after alias \"atletico paranaense\"...). Hmm, \"Atletico - ES\", \"Atlético - AC\", \"Atlético - BA\", \"Atlético - GO\" all collapse to \"atletico\" if I don't alias them. That's bad.\n\nBetter: keep the state in the canonical key when the team name itself is generic (i.e., after stripping, the remaining name is one of a set of generic names like \"atletico\", \"america\", \"botafogo\", \"bragantino\", \"vasco\"...). Alternative simpler robust approach: canonical key = accent-folded lowercase of full original name WITH state suffix normalized (\"palmeiras-sp\"), THEN alias-map full names to canonical display names. That avoids collisions entirely:\n\n- key(\"Palmeiras-SP\") = \"palmeiras-sp\"; alias[\"palmeiras-sp\"] = \"Palmeiras\"\n- key(\"Palmeiras\") = \"palmeiras\"; alias[\"palmeiras\"] = \"Palmeiras\"\n- alias maps both to \"Palmeiras\".\n- key(\"Atlético-PR\") = \"atletico-pr\" → alias → \"Athletico Paranaense\"\n- key(\"Atlético - PR\") = normalize spaces → \"atletico - pr\" → hmm need to normalize \" - \" → \"-\". Let me canonicalize: remove spaces around hyphens → \"atletico-pr\". Good.\n- key(\"Athletico Paranaense\") = \"athletico paranaense\" → alias → \"Athletico Paranaense\"\n- key(\"Athletico\") = \"athletico\" → alias → \"Athletico Paranaense\" (in Libertadores, \"Athletico\" alone refers to Athletico Paranaense)\n- key(\"Atletico - ES\") = \"atletico-es\" → not in alias → fallback: strip state suffix → \"atletico\" + we keep \"es\"? For obscure teams fallback key could be \"atletico (es)\" or just leave \"atletico-es\" as the key. Leave as \"atletico-es\" — no collision with \"atletico-ac\". \n\nSo the general rule:\n- canonical_key = accent_fold(lower(original)) with spaces around \"-\" collapsed, parens content removed (except keep if it doesn't look like a country code? simpler: remove \"(antigo ...)\" type parenthetical always for the key, but for foreign country codes \"(URU)\" also remove — but then \"Nacional (URU)\" → \"nacional\" and \"Nacional-URU\"? no such thing. Fine.)\n- Then strip trailing state suffix ONLY if the result (with alias) is a known team. Actually simpler: alias lookup first on full key; if miss, strip \"-xx\" state suffix and look up alias again; if miss, keep stripped name as key... but that reintroduces the atletico collision for unaliased teams: \"atletico-es\" → strip → \"atletico\". Collision with... \"Athletico\" → aliased to paranaense. \"Atlético - AC\" → \"atletico-ac\" → strip → \"atletico\". Collision \"atletico-es\" vs \"atletico-ac\" vs \"atletico-ba\" — all would become \"atletico\". Are those teams in multiple files? \"Atletico - ES\" (Cup), \"Atlético - AC\" (Cup), \"Atlético - BA\" (Cup) — all in Copa do Brasil only. Within the same file, would collide. Risk: Atletico-ES appears multiple times (2-leg ties), merged with Atletico-AC matches — wrong data.\n\nSafest fallback: if alias misses after stripping, use the FULL key WITH state suffix as the canonical key (i.e., don't strip). So:\n1. key = fold(original)\n2. if key in ALIAS: canonical = ALIAS[key]\n3. elif key ends with \"-<state>\" and fold(stripped) in ALIAS or stripped is a well-known team: canonical = ALIAS.get(stripped, stripped)\n4. else: canonical = key (full, with suffix)\n\nWait, but for step 3 I need care: \"palmeiras-sp\" → strip → \"palmeiras\" → alias? Not in alias needed; use \"palmeiras\" as canonical. And \"atletico-es\" → strip → \"atletico\" → hmm, this would wrongly strip. How do I know \"palmeiras\" is a complete team name but \"atletico\" is not? I can't perfectly. \n\nPragmatic approach: alias map covers the well-known teams (the ~40 Brasileirão clubs + common cup variants). For any key (with or without suffix), if after stripping the suffix the stripped name is in KNOWN_TEAMS set (built from alias values + names that appear WITHOUT suffix anywhere), use the stripped canonical. Otherwise keep full key with suffix. \"atletico\" alone appears in data as \"Athletico\" (Libertadores) → aliased to \"Athletico Paranaense\". So \"atletico\" would NOT be in the fallback known set unless I add it... Since \"Athletico\" is aliased to \"Athletico Paranaense\", after alias resolution the value set contains \"athletico paranaense\". \"atletico\" (stripped from atletico-es) is not equal to \"athletico paranaense\", and not in known set → keep \"atletico-es\". \n\nBut \"palmeiras\" IS in known set because \"Palmeiras\" appears without suffix in Libertadores and BR-Football files. \n\nImplementation: \n- ALIAS dict maps folded variants → canonical display name (e.g., \"atletico-pr\"→\"Athletico Paranaense\", \"athletico\"→\"Athletico Paranaense\", \"vasco\"→\"Vasco da Gama\", \"vasco da gama\"→\"Vasco da Gama\", \"vasco da gama-rj\"→\"Vasco da Gama\", \"vasco da gama rj\"→\"Vasco da Gama\", \"sport\"→\"Sport Recife\", \"sport-pe\"→\"Sport Recife\", \"sport recife\"→\"Sport Recife\", \"csa-al\"→\"CSA\", \"csa\"→\"CSA\", \"c.r.b. - AL\"→\"CRB\", \"c. r. b.\"→\"CRB\" ...).\n- CANONICAL set = set of canonical display names (folded) that appear as standalone names in the data (I'll seed with the values of ALIAS plus a static list of the 20-40 classic clubs).\n- resolve(name):\n  - clean spaces, fold accents → k\n  - if k in ALIAS → return\n  - if k matches r\"-(state)$\" (after collapsing spaces around dash) → s = k[:-3]; if s in ALIAS → return; if s in CANONICAL → return canonical\n  - return k\n\nAlso handle \"Sao Jose - POA\" — POA isn't a state (Porto Alegre city). Folded: \"sao jose - poa\" → \"sao jose-poa\" → not state suffix → stays as full key. But BR-Football also has \"Sao Jose RS\" — different key → treated as different teams. Minor, acceptable. Actually \"Sao Jose-RS\" vs \"Sao Jose RS\"... I could add a rule: trailing state name too (\"RS\" appended w/o dash). \"Sao Jose RS\" → strip \" rs\"? Only if stripped result in CANONICAL. \"sao jose\" not in CANONICAL → keep. Fine.\n\nDisplay names: keep the most common original form for display. I'll track counts of original names per canonical key and pick the most frequent as display name. That gives e.g. \"São Paulo\" (novo file) vs \"Sao Paulo\" (BR file) → pick most frequent. \n\nDates:\n- \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\" → datetime\n- \"29/03/2003\" → date\n- \"2023-09-24\" → date\n- Brazilian_Cup datetime unquoted: \"2012-03-07 16:00:00\"\n- Some rows may have empty goals (walkover). Check: Libertadores home_goal is string \"2\". Possibly empty. Handle missing → None and skip in goal aggregations.\n\nFIFA data: 189 columns; I'll select: ID, Name, Age, Nationality, Overall, Potential, Club, Position, Jersey Number, Height, Weight, Value, Wage, Preferred Foot, Work Rate, plus a few key skills (Finishing, Dribbling, ShortPassing, etc.). Actually simpler: keep the whole row but extract a curated dict. 18k rows × full row = fine in memory. I'll curate ~20 fields.\n\nAlso note FIFA Club values: \"Paris Saint-Germain\", \"FC Barcelona\", and Brazilian clubs like \"Flamengo\"? Let me check quickly what Brazilian clubs appear.\n\nQuery engine tools for MCP:\n1. `search_matches` — filters: team, opponent, date_from, date_to, competition, season, stage/round, limit. Returns list + summary (h2h counts when two teams).\n2. `head_to_head` — team_a, team_b, optional competition/season.\n3. `team_statistics` — team, season?, competition? → matches, W/D/L, GF/GA, home/away split, win rate.\n4. `standings` — competition, season → calculated table.\n5. `search_players` — name?, nationality?, club?, position?, min_overall?, sort_by, limit; plus club summary.\n6. `player_profile` — exact-ish name lookup.\n7. `top_scorers` — hmm, no goal-scorer data in datasets! Can't do top scorers. Skip; standings instead. The spec says \"Top scorers (if inferable from data)\" — not inferable. Skip.\n8. `competition_overview` / `compare_seasons` — aggregate stats: avg goals, home win rate, biggest wins.\n9. `biggest_wins` — limit, competition.\n10. `list_teams` / `list_competitions` maybe.\n\nAlso a generic `ask` tool? No — keep structured tools; the LLM picks.\n\nResources: maybe expose dataset summary as MCP resources. Nice-to-have: a `data://summary` resource. Keep small.\n\nPerformance: load CSVs at startup (~23k match rows + 18k players) — fast with csv module (<2s).\n\nTests: BDD-style pytest tests mirroring the Gherkin scenarios + coverage of each capability area, team name normalization, date parsing, performance smoke (<2s lookups), cross-file queries.\n\nLet me check FIFA clubs for Brazilian clubs and check goal field anomalies.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05943a62d001WX58kwFxofWD7A","sessionID":"ses_fa6bfda21ffejsIdYbHxpf8aCL","messageID":"msg_059427d93001k6A8BA4jIldiIX"}}
