nical teams, same score) preferring the primary source. That handles the \"15 more matches\" example nicely. I'll implement dedupe in search_matches with default True.\n\nDate parsing:\n- \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\", \"%Y-%m-%d\", \"%d/%m/%Y\", maybe \"%d/%m/%Y %H:%M\"\n- return (date, datetime)\n\nnovo file: Data \"29/03/2003\", Ano 2003, Rodada, Vencedor, Arena. Note: rows may have half-time data? No. Also duplicate round entries? Fine.\n\nGoals: int parse; if empty → None.\n\nFIFA player record: curated fields: id, name, age, nationality, overall, potential, club (resolved canonical + raw), position, jersey_number, preferred_foot, work_rate, value, wage, height, weight, joined, contract_valid_until, and top skills: crossing, finishing, heading_accuracy, short_passing, volleys, dribbling, curve, fk_accuracy, long_passing, ball_control, acceleration, sprint_speed, agility, reactions, balance? Let me just include a solid subset: Crossing, Finishing, HeadingAccuracy, ShortPassing, Volleys, Dribbling, Curve, FKAccuracy, LongPassing, BallControl, Acceleration, SprintSpeed, Agility, Reactions, Balance, ShotPower, Jumping, Stamina, Strength, LongShots, Aggression, Interceptions, Positioning, Vision, Penalties, Composure, Marking, StandingTackle, SlidingTackle, GKDiving, GKHandling, GKKicking, GKPositioning, GKReflexes — that's a lot; include all, they're just dict entries. Actually simpler: keep the full raw row too? 18k × 89 fields = memory heavy (~9MB CSV → maybe 60MB in Python dicts). Curated ~40 fields is fine, or full is fine too on modern machines. I'll curate.\n\nHeight \"5'7\" → convert to cm? Provide raw. Weight \"159lbs\" → raw. Keep raw strings, provide as-is.\n\nValue \"€110.5M\" — keep raw.\n\nqueries.py — the engine:\n\n```python\nclass SoccerKnowledgeBase:\n    def __init__(self, data_dir): load all\n    matches: list[Match]\n    players: list[Player]\n    team_names: dict[canonical -> display]\n```\n\nMethods:\n1. find_team(name) → canonical or None (exact canonical, alias, or fuzzy: folded substring unique match)\n2. search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, round=None, limit=50, dedupe=True)\n   - team: matches where team is home or away; opponent: other side\n3. head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None, season=None) → matches + summary counts\n4. team_statistics(team, season=None, competition=None) → played, W/D/L overall + home/away, GF/GA, win rates; per-competition breakdown when not filtered\n5. standings(competition, season) → table computed from chosen source: P, W, D, L, GF, GA, GD, Pts (3/1/0), position, champion note. Which source? Among sources containing that competition label & season, pick the one with the most matches for that season (completeness heuristic), report source.\n   - Note: novo file covers 2003-2019 Série A; Brasileirao_Matches 2012-2023. For 2019 both have 380. Tie → prefer Brasileirao_Matches (primary spec file). Also points: all Série A 2003+ use 3 pts. Note relegation: bottom 4 in Série A (since 2006-ish) — include a note \"last 4 relegated\" for Série A seasons ≥2007? The spec example asks \"Which teams were relegated in 2020?\" → standings bottom 4. I'll add relegation_note for Série A: bottom 4 (2007+; before that bottom 4 in 2003-2006 also 4? In 2003-2005 it was bottom... 2003: 2 relegated? Actually 2003 Série A: 4 relegated (24 teams → 22 in 2004? no...). Ugh. Keep it simple: mark bottom 4 as \"relegation zone\" for Série A with a caveat in text.\n6. search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_age=None, sort='overall', limit=25)\n   - nationality normalized (\"brazil\" matches \"Brazil\")\n   - club matched on raw club substring OR canonical key equality\n   - name substring (case/accent-insensitive)\n7. player_profile(name) → best match + full attributes\n8. players_at_club(club) → grouped summary (count, avg rating, top 5) — like example format\n9. competition_summary(competition, season=None) → n matches, date range, avg goals, home win %, draw %, biggest win, top scoring teams\n10. biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)\n11. compare_seasons(competition, season_a, season_b) → aggregates\n12. list_teams(competition=None) → canonical team list w/ display names\n13. cross-file: not a separate tool — e.g., \"players_at_club\" + matches. Maybe one tool `team_overview(team)` that combines: matches count by competition, players in FIFA for that club, h2h rivals. Nice for cross-file success criterion. I'll add `team_overview`.\n\nMCP server tools (each returns formatted markdown text):\n- search_matches(team, opponent, competition, season, date_from, date_to, stage, round, limit)\n- head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition, season, limit)\n- team_statistics(team, season, competition)\n- standings(competition, season)\n- search_players(name, nationality, club, position, min_overall, max_overall, max_age, sort, limit)\n- player_profile(name)\n- team_overview(team)\n- competition_statistics(competition, season)\n- biggest_wins(competition, season, limit)\n- compare_seasons(competition, season_a, season_b)\n- list_teams(competition)\n- Plus a resource: data summary.\n\nServer: use MCPServer from mcp 2.x. Entry: `main()` → `mcp.run(transport='stdio')`.\n\nCheck how MCPServer.run works with stdio — `mcp.run('stdio')`. And tools registered via `@mcp.tool()` decorator. In mcp 2.x the tool decorator registers via add_tool. Let me verify quickly that the decorator works and the tool call works in a test (use Client from mcp 2.x? There's `mcp.client` presumably). For tests, I'll test the KB layer directly and do an in-process MCP test using the server's list_tools/call_tool methods if simple. Let me check mcp 2.x client API availability for an end-to-end stdio test. `mcp.client.fastmcp`? Let me check.\n\nThen tests/test_bdd.py: pytest BDD style:\n- fixture kb (session scope) loading from data dir\n- Gherkin-scenario-mapped tests with Given/When/Then comments\n- Cover: match search (Fla-Flu, Palmeiras 2023, Copa do Brasil finals), team stats (Corinthians 2022 home record), h2h (Palmeiras vs Santos), players (Brazilian players, club players, positions), standings (2019 champion = Flamengo 90 pts? verify: Flamengo 2019: 28W 6D 4L = 90 pts ✓ per spec example — good validation target), biggest wins, avg goals, date normalization, team name variations, performance (<2s lookups, <5s aggregates), cross-file (team_overview).\n\nCheck 2019 champion from Brasileirao_Matches to make sure the test expectation matches data. Let me compute quickly after writing loader. I'll write tests based on actual computed data (verify Flamengo champion 2019 in this dataset).\n\nAlso root server.py launcher + requirements.txt + README section. Also `.gitignore`? Not needed.\n\nLet me now check mcp 2.x client for end-to-end testing.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05946aa73001HYYNZ7e2aS34J0","sessionID":"ses_fa6bfda21ffejsIdYbHxpf8aCL","messageID":"msg_05944e856001jBoHMQEJk1shAC"}}
