{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788204442568,"sessionID":"ses_fa6bfda21ffejsIdYbHxpf8aCL","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_1e643393fd29466d85757c77","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"venv/bin/python -c \"\nfrom bsoccer.data_loader import load_all\nfrom collections import Counter\nmatches, players = load_all()\ncup = [m for m in matches if m.source_file=='Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv']\nprint('cup rounds:', Counter(m.round for m in cup))\nlib = [m for m in matches if m.source_file=='Libertadores_Matches.csv']\nprint('lib stages:', Counter(m.stage for m in lib))\n\""},"output":"cup rounds: Counter({'2': 376, '1': 334, '3': 258, '4': 140, '5': 123, '6': 62, '7': 30, '8': 14})\nlib stages: Counter({'group stage': 960, 'round of 16': 160, 'quarterfinals': 80, 'semifinals': 40, 'final': 15})\n","metadata":{"output":"cup rounds: Counter({'2': 376, '1': 334, '3': 258, '4': 140, '5': 123, '6': 62, '7': 30, '8': 14})\nlib stages: Counter({'group stage': 960, 'round of 16': 160, 'quarterfinals': 80, 'semifinals': 40, 'final': 15})\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"venv/bin/python -c \"\nfrom bsoccer.data_loader import load_all\nfrom collections import Counter\nmatches, players = load_all()\ncup = [m for m in matches if m.source_file=='Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv']\nprint('cup rounds:', Counter(m.round for m in cup))\nlib = [m for m in matches if m.source_file=='Libertadores_Matches.csv']\nprint('lib stages:', Counter(m.stage for m in lib))\n\"","time":{"start":1788204442553,"end":1788204442567}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The alias coverage looks excellent. There are a few more multi-spelling canonicals I could add aliases for (accent variants of small cup teams like 'Aguia Negra', 'Luziania', 'Operario'), but those only differ in accents and already unify because folding handles accents — wait, no: 'Aguia Negra - MS' and 'Águia Negra - MS' both fold to 'aguia negra-ms' → same canonical ✓ already unified (that's why they're grouped). Those are fine — folding did its job. The remaining merges are correct ones.\n\nOne question: 'Red Bull Brasil' merged with 'Red Bull Bragantino' — RB Brasil was rebranded to RB Bragantino in 2019 (Copa do Brasil: Red Bull Brasil entries in 2019 were actually the same club transitioning). Slight historical inaccuracy (2018 RB Brasil was separate from Bragantino which was relegated in Série B... actually the merger happened in 2019/2020; \"Red Bull Brasil\" in the 2019+ cup data IS the club that became Red Bull Bragantino). Acceptable for a demo. Hmm, but to be safe let me NOT merge 'Red Bull Brasil' with Bragantino... In the Copa do Brasil data 2019-2020, \"Red Bull Brasil\" played. Clube Atlético Bragantino (old) became Red Bull Brasil's B-team. For a demo knowledge graph this merge is defensible. Keep.\n\nAlso \"Guarani-SP\" → 'guarani-sp' — distinct from 'Guaraní (PAR)' ✓. And plain \"Guarani\" from novo → 'guarani' standalone canonical ✓ distinct.\n\nNow check remaining distinct-team integrity: verify that e.g. \"Botafogo-PB\" didn't merge with \"Botafogo\" (not in suspicious list → they're separate ✓ since Botafogo-PB raw would appear under 'botafogo-pb' canonical). \n\nAlso FIFA clubs resolve: 'ceara sporting club' → 'Ceará' ✓ via alias; 'sport club do recife' → 'Sport Recife' ✓.\n\nNow write queries.py. Design:\n\n```python\nclass KnowledgeBase:\n    def __init__(self, data_dir=None):\n        self.matches, self.players = load_all(data_dir)\n        self._team_display: dict[str, str] — canonical -> nicest display\n        self._by_competition index? Just filter lists; 24k items scan is ~ms.\n```\n\nFor find_team: resolve raw input; if resolved equals some canonical in dataset → OK. Also fuzzy: if exact canonical not found, look for canonical keys containing the folded input; unique match → use it; multiple → return None? Return best? For tools, return list of candidates. I'll implement `find_team(name) -> str | None` with fuzzy contains; ambiguous → pick shortest (usually the intended). Hmm, ambiguity: \"América\" contains → 'América Mineiro', 'América de Natal'. Pick... I'd rather return None and let tool list candidates. I'll implement find_team returning (canonical, candidates).\n\nCompetition resolution: map user string → set of competition labels. Fold both. Rules:\n- contains 'brasileir' or ('serie' & 'a' exact-ish)... Let me define label sets:\n  COMPETITION_GROUPS:\n  - 'brasileirao': labels {Brasileirão Série A}\n  - 'serie-a': {Brasileirão Série A, Série A (BR-Football)}\n  - 'serie-b': {Série B (BR-Football)}\n  - 'serie-c': {Série C (BR-Football)}\n  - 'copa do brasil': {Copa do Brasil, Copa do Brasil (BR-Football)}\n  - 'libertadores': {Copa Libertadores}\n  Matching: fold(user query) → find group whose key words match. I'll implement: for each known label, if all significant words of the user query appear in folded label → match. Simpler: check 'brasileir' in q → both 'Brasileirão Série A'; elif 'libertadores' in q → libertadores; elif 'copa do brasil' in q or 'copa-brasil'... elif 'serie a'/'serie-a' → serie-a; etc.\n\nI'll write a resolver returning list of matching competition labels (data labels). \n\nFunctions (all take/return plain dicts for MCP friendliness):\n\n- search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, stage=None, round=None, limit=50, dedupe=True) → dict with matches list, count, h2h summary if team+opponent.\n- head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None, season=None, limit=50)\n- team_statistics(team, season=None, competition=None)\n- standings(competition, season) — picks source: among (source_file, competition-label) pairs with that season and competition group match, choose the source with most matches that season; prefer primary sources on tie. Compute table. Mark champion + relegation note for Brasileirão Série A (bottom 4).\n- search_players(...)\n- player_profile(name)\n- club_players(club) — grouped: count, avg overall, top players (the example format \"Flamengo: 8 players (avg rating: 74)\")\n- competition_statistics(competition=None, season=None) — n matches, avg goals, home/draw/away win %, biggest win, date range, teams count\n- biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)\n- compare_seasons(competition, season_a, season_b)\n- team_overview(team) — cross-file: matches by competition, records, FIFA players at that club\n- list_teams(competition=None)\n- summary() — dataset summary for the resource\n\nFormatting: return structured dicts; server layer renders markdown. Actually to keep server thin, queries return dicts and a formatter module... I'll put a `formatting.py`? Keep it in queries with small helpers returning markdown strings AND structured data? The MCP tools will return markdown text (good for LLM). I'll have queries return dicts, and server.py format them. Better separation: `bsoccer/formatting.py` with format_* functions. Hmm — to limit file count, I'll do: queries.py returns rich dicts; server.py has formatting inline. Tests test the dict layer mostly + a couple of formatted outputs.\n\nDedupe logic: key = (date, sorted teams, home_goal, away_goal). Keep first occurrence with source priority order: Brasileirao_Matches > Brazilian_Cup_Matches > Libertadores_Matches > novo > BR-Football. Note two-legged cup ties have same teams and score? Same date required — two legs on different dates ✓. But same date same teams same score duplicates exist in Libertadores? \"2-2 2013-02-12 Nacional vs Barcelona\" unique. Fine.\n\nRound filtering: round values in cup are '1','2',...; in brasileirao '1'..'38'; novo Rodada '1'..'38'. stage filter for Libertadores ('group stage', 'final', etc.). Let me check stage values for finals: \"Find all Copa do Brasil finals\" — round values for finals in cup file? Let me check quickly.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0594a60c0001pxE6OuTAcjtOJQ","sessionID":"ses_fa6bfda21ffejsIdYbHxpf8