RITY.get(m.source_file, 99))\n+    self.matches_unique = self._dedupe(ordered)\n \n # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n # Name resolution\n # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n","additions":3,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"bsoccer/queries.py","time":{"start":1788205584798,"end":1788205584800}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Also `list_teams` counts would double-count cross-file duplicates. Let me apply dedupe there too — but dedupe is per-match-list; list_teams iterates all matches globally. Dedupe all matches once at KB build time? That would change search_matches behavior (already dedupes) and standings (single-source). A globally deduped list is actually the cleanest: build `self.matches_deduped` once, use in team_overview, list_teams, competition_statistics, biggest_wins, head_to_head. But careful: global dedupe requires a deterministic priority order across ALL matches (I sort by source priority in _dedupe only within input order... my _dedupe keeps the FIRST occurrence unless a later one has higher priority — so order matters. For global dedupe, pre-sort by source priority then dedupe).\n\nActually simpler: in `_build_indexes`, build `self.matches_unique` = dedupe(self.matches sorted by priority). Then:\n- team_overview, list_teams, competition_statistics, biggest_wins, head_to_head use matches_unique\n- search_matches keeps its own per-query dedupe (fine)\n- standings unchanged (single source)\n- summary: count both raw and unique.\n\nLet me implement that, replacing the per-method _dedupe calls with the precomputed list where sensible.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0595bd197001RAYGJihhCMc9MC","sessionID":"ses_fa6bfda21ffejsIdYbHxpf8aCL","messageID":"msg_0595b8373001Irx7soKBhC7eQW"}}
